        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "While Muslim armies were achieving military success, rival groups fought for the caliphate, or the office of caliph. The struggle began when Ali, Muhammad’s son-in-law, became the fourth caliph in A.D. 656. One of Ali’s powerful rivals was Mu’awiyah (moo•UH•wee•uh), the governor of Syria. Mu’awiyah carried out conquests in Egypt and Iraq, steadily weakening Ali’s hold on the caliphate. In A.D. 661, Ali was murdered by a disillusioned follower, and Mu’awiyah became the first caliph of the powerful Umayyad (oo•MY•uhd) dynasty. Ali’s son Husayn (hoo•SAYN), however, refused to accept Umayyad rule and continued the struggle. In A.D. 680, Husayn and many of his followers were massacred by Umayyad troops in a battle at Karbala in present-day Iraq.",
          "block_bbox": [
